explanation:
The body builds the vector whose coordinate j is:
- v_j when j < k;
- √(1 − ‖v‖²) when j = k;
- 0 otherwise.

This is exactly the map the docstring describes, and the standard map used to build the cells of ℝPⁿ. The lemmas next to it check the expected properties:
- `norm_hemispherePoint`: the image lies on the unit sphere when k ≤ n and ‖v‖ ≤ 1;
- `continuous_hemispherePoint`: the map is continuous;
- a further lemma: the map is odd on the boundary sphere.

Outside the intended domain (‖v‖ > 1, where Real.sqrt gives 0, or k > n, where the coordinate k is dropped) the values are harmless junk.

The map is not too narrow. It is a specific map in coordinates, and the coordinate setting is what this construction (the CW structure on ℝPⁿ via the inclusions ℝᵏ ⊆ ℝⁿ⁺¹) needs. The docstring also connects it to the general coordinate-free lift y ↦ y + √(1−‖y‖²)·p, which is behind `TauCeti.hemisphereHomeomorph`. Mathlib has no coordinate version of this map.
